#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221115 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "c953f344-77dd-5eaa-9f57-33626a392041" :question/math-academy-id "e-21665" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above." :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0.$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/assets/9cff385cda8e9a4cac784cb4212fee9ec1e65f48a1ebd05f9af75f71aead66aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221116 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/id #uuid "e7405495-e774-5508-b22b-e72d82eb4346" :question/math-academy-id "e-21666" :question/problem "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\n- Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and sometimes converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ there exists a neighborhood $O_1 \\subseteq O$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O_1$ at some point in time $t=t_0$ remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a3802a9-0f54-53e4-8f51-5dd89c864e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Solutions converge to the equilibrium only if the system is linear." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359437} {:answer/id #uuid "19426713-b39c-53f9-99cf-6429cbc787c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359438} {:answer/id #uuid "b6dd0d14-19d1-562f-aa3e-ac0e4eb03323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359439} {:answer/id #uuid "da013dea-3275-5479-9c64-6fb363497cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Solutions approach the equilibrium only along specific trajectories." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359440} {:answer/id #uuid "78164657-caae-5d95-91d8-de016269a117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19426713-b39c-53f9-99cf-6429cbc787c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359438} :answer-field/id #uuid "b35eed4d-7668-54c5-ad4e-fb519364521c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b6b058e-9ff1-5f78-9443-7901f71ac9f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332968" :question/problem "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fc71ead-7e89-5167-bf85-550e4a6e99e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359443} {:answer/id #uuid "666e0ff7-33f9-5005-8ef5-eb03ad305c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359444} {:answer/id #uuid "88f796b8-628e-57bf-8c52-1c57678c15e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359445} {:answer/id #uuid "412d02da-d8de-5b76-9883-b9292a63e71c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359446} {:answer/id #uuid "1a200f94-9fab-56dd-8824-e6c3e2e8797a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fc71ead-7e89-5167-bf85-550e4a6e99e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359443} :answer-field/id #uuid "b665653f-5025-5f24-bb8b-df34a10441a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eda104b-fb43-5560-b213-0b2f03bde928" :question/math-academy-id "q-333360" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ 2x-y-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3y-3=0\\,⇒\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(-1)+1=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c549d0ed-1c30-5a3a-b716-f720ad9aa9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Unstable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220337} {:answer/id #uuid "d07e7a83-3509-5d50-b258-7b5d720fec78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable only for certain initial conditions." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220338} {:answer/id #uuid "01bf4b62-ca90-52d9-ae81-52349884e401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically unstable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220339} {:answer/id #uuid "1231d677-4871-51fa-b89f-2fde1c562f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220340} {:answer/id #uuid "548bc11d-36ec-562d-921e-af30373bf967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Stable but not asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c549d0ed-1c30-5a3a-b716-f720ad9aa9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Unstable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220337} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e5af361-3180-5bd7-aef9-d46cae03651f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b4d4a22-e4b9-59fa-a122-f0172b49e4a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333385" :question/problem "A small perturbation away from an equilibrium causes trajectories to slowly spiral away from the equilibrium. How should the equilibrium be classified?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Unstable.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c86631ba-5345-569f-85c1-a80b34b80210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359450} {:answer/id #uuid "a5afa473-578e-5078-b850-3d0a17657cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c86631ba-5345-569f-85c1-a80b34b80210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359450} :answer-field/id #uuid "3517b8ea-67d1-585b-b3ba-b6be985baf15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359449} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54319469-2674-5653-b925-ae0a442a6905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "and converge to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359453} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6fdb49-6501-5960-ab6e-e12ebabdb84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "but may not converge to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359454} {:answer/id #uuid "3331fc98-4779-5ae7-a4b7-1c92d49e0ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "but move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54319469-2674-5653-b925-ae0a442a6905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "and converge to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359453} :answer-field/id #uuid "5efa9825-5398-55a7-af82-e5b8a7cd77f6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cc3739f-7687-51cb-b932-ef3925528e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359457} {:answer/id #uuid "8a503737-678c-5088-bdb1-d5f2a2bcec00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "equilibrium" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a503737-678c-5088-bdb1-d5f2a2bcec00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "equilibrium" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359458} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c5c1b57-08a7-5c7f-9cd0-c7592a11db5f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cbc2a80-9ed0-54b1-9d3f-63cf07946d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\to \\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359460} {:answer/id #uuid "7fcd93d8-eb4d-527d-aadd-67fc4e4e8553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\to 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cbc2a80-9ed0-54b1-9d3f-63cf07946d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\to \\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359460} :answer-field/id #uuid "be17fc75-fd6d-57cd-89a4-717b135a1d68" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3889c218-203d-525e-b513-cc653db33652" :question/math-academy-id "q-335458" :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point.The solutions that start sufficiently \n{{field-1}}\nthe equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium\n{{field-2}} \nthe \n{{field-3}} \nas \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently $\\text{close to}$ the equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium $\\text{and converge to}$ the $\\text{equilibrium}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beb7bdeb-3698-506a-a17a-649ec6a473e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359464} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd3f73b-6552-5376-a1b4-6c2dead84c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cd3f73b-6552-5376-a1b4-6c2dead84c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359465} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a12cfc8-d704-5483-a81b-4422b2e0187e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359463} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5449319-7870-58a0-aff3-bb57740002c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "arbitrarily close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359467} {:answer/id #uuid "3500047e-2f42-5664-a968-66fcf2aab66d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "far from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5449319-7870-58a0-aff3-bb57740002c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "arbitrarily close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359467} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0dcab76-0870-5503-be1f-3635a35b5878" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359466} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e23e98ad-aaa7-5b25-a153-f9483a81e08c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "remain close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359470} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9c0a1e-2249-5209-85a0-854d3d6d7dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "eventually move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c9c0a1e-2249-5209-85a0-854d3d6d7dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "eventually move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359471} :answer-field/id #uuid "49bfdbba-117c-5ca8-98ef-0fc9f3ac2c0d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4f31534-294a-5c66-bb7a-bbd9c8ed5e89" :question/math-academy-id "q-335460" :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **unstable** equilibrium point.{{field-1}} solutions that start\n{{field-2}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-3}}\nthe equilibrium." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n$\\text{Some}$ solutions that start $\\text{arbitrarily close to}$ the equilibrium $\\text{eventually move away from}$ the equilibrium."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db6c711e-a164-50b5-bfec-66eb3cd983c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable only for certain initial conditions." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359474} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ffea3e-b54a-599a-a784-6d10da09dad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Unstable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359475} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1a3bdf-2e9a-55ce-80ff-de9408d3d51b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359476} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ca0e9b-d61e-5c9e-a3e6-56e9bc0fbccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Stable but not asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359477} {:answer/id #uuid "22ff3695-5149-575b-9a4a-78208dae0586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable only for nonlinear systems." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0ca0e9b-d61e-5c9e-a3e6-56e9bc0fbccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Stable but not asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359477} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac1cde9-39f2-5443-a24a-e79a254128b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2b4530a-6c6c-5e8b-9d00-bf27eb10da96" :question/math-academy-id "q-335465" :question/problem "A small perturbation away from an equilibrium causes trajectories to remain in a bounded region around the equilibrium, sometimes oscillating or circling, but they do not converge to the equilibrium as time increases. How should the equilibrium be classified?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Stable but not asymptotically stable.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fde1925-6b36-5327-ab25-206019e09fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359481} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4d2050-7c44-5f30-b92e-356be9d07fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359482} {:answer/id #uuid "290f356d-3f9b-554d-804c-f131cd0fd4f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359483} {:answer/id #uuid "1a301477-8cae-5d27-8b48-f50181e7e9bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359484} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9306af-9f87-58ec-8c7f-fdbe49283a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe4d2050-7c44-5f30-b92e-356be9d07fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359482} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2a16de5-d93c-5668-b2d1-ff3a5faa12ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eac2bde7-7ca3-515f-8106-3f78aefe257b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335470" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-1=0 \\\\ 2x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding both equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n3x-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+2y-1=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e78d487-79b6-5de2-b365-3c200797c2aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359488} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0a19de-d64b-5849-abd5-8c6343460b3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359489} {:answer/id #uuid "11af0954-29ac-5c4b-b021-fca42a152f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359490} {:answer/id #uuid "7de20d88-bc1f-5916-af50-3eb6cc255eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359491} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf93f14-563c-5796-9154-0c5613daf3d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7de20d88-bc1f-5916-af50-3eb6cc255eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359491} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb26ac33-1571-50cd-aa57-eb7e5c01f475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a345a07a-9a32-5621-bbdf-168369d7d55d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335558" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ x(4x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=2\\cdot 0=0.$\n\n- For $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get $y=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b2ca989-a425-5592-88a4-4344cd27baa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359495} {:answer/id #uuid "f99bad36-7823-5012-bce2-7e328a078b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359496} {:answer/id #uuid "00a33ccb-ca43-50bb-9c6a-bb6eab28778b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359497} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf3776d-50e7-5d41-9c95-9b45ee960d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359498} {:answer/id #uuid "1083150c-e35a-5ea8-b568-d52bcd09d65f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b2ca989-a425-5592-88a4-4344cd27baa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359495} :answer-field/id #uuid "996a4a93-c6e4-56cc-9cf4-1121328d5678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb0dc9ee-4198-5fbc-84d6-225dac676eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-335639" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 3x-y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-7y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3678b52-6f52-5320-96b5-d3d645efa19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359502} {:answer/id #uuid "c29437a1-c9a1-5b80-930f-82c8b92cd15e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359503} {:answer/id #uuid "9517018c-c2e8-5e95-8d60-013c65af69cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359504} {:answer/id #uuid "963c966c-8b46-5e69-88de-454c40b1cdac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359505} {:answer/id #uuid "112090d6-364f-5200-8be1-74b0719229b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3678b52-6f52-5320-96b5-d3d645efa19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359502} :answer-field/id #uuid "73a08da2-0d7c-561b-a1c9-95024bd0944d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38dcf5d3-98d5-5fe3-9e27-ff6452649b0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335646" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y=0 \\\\ x+y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-5y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
